st on basis of the band size  so what you do in Western blot  you take the gel and for example this is  your you know this is the band of your  interest but it might be some other  proteins as well so what you do is like  you put it in a transfer apparatus where  you put the SDS gel and on top of which  you put PVDF membrane and you transfer  either by chemically or by electrical  field so most popular is electrical  field or electro bond blood these days  so as a result what will happen the  protein is that right now on the jail  would be eventually transferred and  being on the membrane so now once we  have all the proteins in the membrane we  can start to detect our protein of  interest by antibody specific manner so  we need an antibody which can detect our  protein of interest and we have to  understand that that antibody is  monoclonal and it's non not specific to  any other protein it is very specific to  the my protein of interest now on the  membrane there would be several protein  brands and several proteins right so all  of these proteins are present in the  lice it that we have taken now we have  to detect the protein of our interest  that is this particular protein where  the antibody is binding so we would only  get a band in that region  so once the antibody binds we can use  several kilometer Akemi luminescence or  fluorescence based method to detect the  particular protein of interest and most  popular and way of detection these days  are chem illuminants now Western Bart  could be used for diagnosis of several  diseases one big example is AIDS so  let's just take a look how it works so  for example we suspect a patient is  infected by HIV virus so we take the  blood sample of that patient we extract  the proteins and we run it onto a tail  now after that we after running the gel  we take  we blot it and try to detect that our  suspected antigen is present or not for  HIV there are known biomarkers like for  example gp120 is one of the suspected  antigen that has to be present if the  patient is infected by HIV so we have to  blot against that particular protein and  if we see a positive man that means the  patient is infected so for example HIV  virus has gp120 and G p12 against gp120  we could have specific antibodies now we  can detect that specific antibodies in  any of chemiluminescent or colorimetric  way and that would give us distinct band  if we get a man that means the patient  is affected if we don't get a man the  test is negative now not only in  diagnosis or medical usage Western blot  is widely used for studying fundamental  scientific research so in basic cell  signaling could be studied by Western  blot for example let's say imagine our  receptor is activated one muscle again  this bound and there are cascade of  events and most important event is  phosphorylation of these blue protein  so once the phosphorylation of the blue  protein takes place then other nuclear  events and downstream even can take  place so this is a crucial event let's  say so these days we have antibodies  specific to the unphosphorylated protein  versus the phosphorylated epitope so the  phospholipid of antibody allows us to  understand how much phosphorylation has  take place and the degree of  phosphorylation would in turn tell us  about the status of the signaling so in  the plot if we get two bands  corresponding to one the phosphor  epitope and one to the normal immature  then we might say the signaling has  turned on or turned off so based on the  situation of the phosphorylation we can  understand the signaling is turned on or  turned off right so these are the  overall usage of Western blot I mean two  basic examples I hope you enjoyed this  video if you liked this video give it a  big thumbs up don't forget to Like share  and subscribe thank you
 

